` handles \"133d21h ago\" and the zero case with \"right now\".\n\nNow I need to update the differential tests—since the reference binary doesn't support ISO 8601, I should keep differential tests only for formats it handles, then add separate unit tests specifically for ISO 8601 validation.\n\nFor the differential suite, I'm keeping tests for syslog, RFC 2822, and lastlog formats, plus cases for no timestamp and empty input. The multiline test should mix syslog and no-timestamp entries without ISO 8601.\n\nFor unit tests, I need one to verify ISO 8601 timestamps are properly matched and replaced, plus the existing exit code tests.\n\nBut wait—the relativeNormalizer function uses `relativeRe.ReplaceAll()` to replace matches with a placeholder, so I need to make sure the regex actually captures what the output produces, not just what the input looks like.\n\nFor the syslog test, the reference output is \"133d21h ago server message\\n\". After normalization, this becomes \"<RELTIME> server message\\n\", and my Go implementation should produce the same relative age format, so the normalized outputs will match. The key is that the regex needs to handle the full pattern including \"ago\" or \"from now\" as part of the relative age string.\n\nNow for the non-differential ISO 8601 test, I need to run the Go binary with the `-r` flag and ISO 8601 input, then verify that the original timestamp has been replaced and the output contains a valid replacement. Writing duration formatting... Refactoring into a single function instead... Writing the duration formatting logic...\n\nNow I'm checking edge cases to make sure the function handles different duration values correctly—like zero duration returning \"right now\", single seconds showing \"1s ago\", and minute values like 60 seconds properly converting to \"1m ago\" with the right unit count. The day and year cases both check out—a full day gives \"1d ago\" with no remainder, and a full year gives \"1y ago\", though I'm noting